<TR><TD valign="top" colspan="2">
The following policy should be viewed more as a guideline than hard facts. At any given time the High Council leads the Souls and therefore can make decisions that may not totally fit with this policy. They will try their best to make decisions only for the good of the Souls and to preserve the friendly harmony in the guild. As Siglo one said "we are a bunch of friends who accidentally levelled" and not a powerguild nor do we follow strict rules. See the Souls as an environment you share with friends and which should help you to improve your EverQuest time so you can enjoy it totally. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD colspan="2"> </TD></TR>
<TR><TD valign="top"><b>1.</b></TD><TD valign="top">
<b>Joining the Souls</b>
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">1.1</TD><TD valign="top">
We are not a recruiting guild. So we do not seek out new members but we are always on the lookout for new friends. So the first step to join us is to clearly seek us out and hunt with us A LOT. Once you became familiar with the Souls and have made some friends you should think about applying to join. On a sidenote the Souls are for the most part set to Eastern Standard Time for schedules like raiding but we still do have a lot of members in other time zones (West Coast, Europe, Australia...). 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">1.2</TD><TD valign="top">
In order to apply to the Souls you will need to go to the message board and make an account that will have the name of your MAIN character (Please note that you do not use any surname because this is taken care of in the profile in another field!). Once you are done with that, you post your application in the "JOINING THE SOULS" forum. Your post should introduce yourself to the Souls who don't know you and it is not a bad idea to even put in some basic information of the RL person behind your character like age, time zone you live in and so on. Make sure you also include why you want to join us and how you came to it. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">1.3</TD><TD valign="top">
Once your application is posted you should continue to meet and group with as many souls as possible. We require you to be known by a majority of our members before you will be able to join our ranks. An internal discussion about your application will take place at the same time where members share their experiences with you. If the overall experience is positive your chances of acceptance are good. If it is more negative you will not be able to join us. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">1.4</TD><TD valign="top">
To be able to join you will also need at least two High Council officers or two Invite Council Officers and one High Council officer who agree to an invite. So you should make sure that you do introduce yourself to the officers in the game as well. If even one officer strongly vetos your application to join you will not be able to. The officers make their decision based upon their own experience with you as well as the reaction of the Souls who meet you. Here is a simple rule: the more friends you make inside the Souls, the higher your chance to join. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">1.5</TD><TD valign="top">
If the officers accept your application you will be invited to the Souls on the following terms. When you enter you begin on a verification status. So if the officers feel you are not fitting in to the souls right from the start you will be asked to remove yourself from the Souls. This period is not fixed! It depends greatly upon whether the officers are able to make a clear decision. An average time frame would be about 3 to 4 weeks total. However you will probably not be informed when the time is over. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">1.6</TD><TD valign="top">
Once you have joined the Souls it is expected of you to follow our guidelines for members. It is very important for you to not abuse any EverQuest rules or harm other players. Meet other players and Souls and offer them the respect you would expect to be met with. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">1.7</TD><TD valign="top">
The Souls do not have a level limit. However the majority of the Souls are Level 50+ so if you are lower in level it might be difficult to find the support you are looking for, both for membership sponsors and for grouping once you have joined. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD colspan="2"> </TD></TR>
<TR><TD valign="top"><b>2.</b></TD><TD valign="top">
<b>Behaviour expected from a Soul</b>
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">2.1</TD><TD valign="top">
At no time is it permissible to harm other players. Neither kill-stealing, training or throwing profanity at another player is acceptable in the EverQuest community. In the case that another player is threatening you, you are asked to leave the trouble to avoid any problems. However in some cases when you think you are doing the right thing, you should contact an officer to help you deal with the other person who is causing you grief. You can also report the player or petition but at no time you should treat him/her like he/she does you! 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">2.2</TD><TD valign="top">
The Souls is a community that endeavours to share everything fairly and equally. It is bad behaviour to whine about loot or experience in either a raid or in normal grouping. The handling of loot is outlined in our <a href='http://www.glaznor.org/index.php?library&id=1'>loot policy</a> and it is expected that you respect the policy. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">2.3</TD><TD valign="top">
It is not recommended to spam the guildchat with questions about how to play the game or price checks. To ask a question is always ok and you should feel free to do so, but asking 20 questions in a row will annoy other players especially when it is 20 price checks. There are various resources on the internet or market channels where those questions can be answered. And it is always good if possible to learn things yourself from experience, rather than asking someone who knows it or looking it up on the web. Knowledge is the key to success in later levels so build it up through research and experimentation. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">2.4</TD><TD valign="top">
Ports and Rezzes are not services to be expected from other Souls. It is nice to help another Soul by porting or rezzing him/her but at no time should you demand it or even expect it. We do not have one rezzer and one porter for each member so it can often be the case that our porters and rezzers are doing something they can't or don't want to drop and you should respect that. They are here to play the game too, not to run around helping others constantly. That said, do not be afraid to ask for a port or rez if you need one. The Souls are generally very helpful toward other members and more often than not you can get help if you need it. Just do not be angry or surprised if no one can drop what they are doing to immediately come help you. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">2.5</TD><TD valign="top">
If you need assistance of another member try to make it as easy as you can for him/her to help you. Like if you want a port try to move yourself to a place he can pick you up easily and quickly (Druid Ring / Wizard Spire) instead of making him run to you. It is not required but recommend to not waste the time of another who may want to do something else and interrupted that to help you. Also realize what you ask for. If you are level 5 and you ask a Soul to rez you even though you can easily access your body, it wont come across very well. The loss of xp is less than the time required to rez you in that case and it is really not a nice way to waste the time of a friend. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">2.6</TD><TD valign="top">
You should participate in what the Souls do. This means you should not log on and hunt without a word while the whole guild is raiding. It is not required to come to every raid but it is also not recommended to stay out of everything we do. Sometimes we all need time away from raiding and helping others but if that is always the case you might think about the reasons you had for joining us. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">2.7</TD><TD valign="top">
Do not expect that as soon as you have joined you are getting priorities on loot or epic raids. Those usually take up a lot of resources from the guild and in general the older members do come first. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">2.8</TD><TD valign="top">
If you encounter problems with other Souls you are asked to bring those to the officers if and only if you have no more ideas left to solve it with the other party directly. It is not advised to start such a discussion openly before consulting an officer. The officer will help to remedy the problem as best they can. In cases where the officers thinks someone is complaining for petty reasons it is highly possible that they will not offer much help though. A legitimate complaint is always one with the will to solve the problem and a possible way how to solve it. Loot complaints are in general not liked by the officers so complain in such cases very politely! 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD colspan="2"> </TD></TR>
<TR><TD valign="top"><b>3.</b></TD><TD valign="top">
<b>Losing membership / leaving</b>
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">3.1</TD><TD valign="top">
If you are leaving the Souls (which will hopefully never happen) it is a nice gesture to let the Souls know why. We surely know that it is not always possible to find the right guild on the first try and you may have other ideals in mind than we have. In such a case it is always better to leave on a friendly note instead of making everyone start to guess why you left. We do not generally like those who leave because they didn't get enough "phat lewt" and in such cases it could easily mean you are not very welcome with us in the future. However we do respect that people have differing personal and guild goals. In such cases it is advised that a member leaves in a polite manner explaining their differences, instead of letting the situation become a problem and forcing guild removal. 
</TD></TR>

<TR><TD valign="top">3.2</TD><TD valign="top">
It is possible that the officers would decide to ask you to leave the Souls. This is something that is usually considered by the officers if they see that you are constantly in trouble with either Souls or other players of the game. The main idea of the Souls is a family of friends, and if the officers feel that you do not fit well with this family or that you are hurting the harmony they will make the decision that you can no longer remain a member. This will in general not be decided by a single officer, even though it is possible very hard cases that one will make the call that you have to leave.
</TD></TR>

